# ===========================================================================
# WHAT NEVER ENTERS THIS REPOSITORY'S HISTORY.
#
# REWRITTEN 5 Sep 2026, AFTER THE FRESH `init`. The repository is built from
# PUBLIC-REPO-TAKE-LIST.txt — nothing ships unless it is named there — and
# PUBLIC-REPO-CONFIG.json's `ignore` list is where every deliberate
# leave-behind is written down, one rule at a time, with the preview printing
# a count of what each rule swallowed. THAT LIST IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH AND
# THIS FILE IS BROUGHT INTO AGREEMENT WITH IT.
#
# ── THE TWO FILES ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, WHICH IS WHY BOTH EXIST ──────
#
# PUBLIC-REPO-CONFIG.json asks: WHAT MAY THE TAKE LIST LEAVE BEHIND WITHOUT A
# DECISION? Anything in this folder that is neither on the take list nor
# matched by a rule there is a REFUSAL — a thing somebody must classify. Its
# rules exist to be counted.
#
# THIS FILE ASKS: WHAT MUST NEVER REACH THE INDEX FROM THIS WORKING FOLDER ON
# THIS MACHINE? That is a superset. It carries editor droppings, local logs
# and scratch names that do not exist in a clone and that the config has no
# reason to name — and it carries them because the cost of a stray file here
# is a commit, not a printed line.
#
# SO: EVERY CONFIG RULE APPEARS HERE. THIS FILE ADDS A FEW AND THE ADDITIONS
# ARE MARKED. Where a config rule is a bare directory name it is written here
# with a trailing `/` so it cannot also swallow a file of that name.
#
# ── WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT DO ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# IGNORING A TRACKED FILE DOES NOTHING. TEN files were in the first commit and
# should not have been — seven documents that should never have shipped, and
# three scripts taken off the take list afterwards. The rules for all ten are
# below AND the removal was UNTRACK-SHIPPED-BY-MISTAKE.command, which is a
# separate act by a person. IT HAS BEEN RUN: none of the ten is in the index
# now, and none was deleted — the seven documents are where they were, and the
# three scripts moved under `.maintainer/probes/` afterwards.
# Every other process file in this folder was never tracked at all — the
# fresh `init` honoured a take list that does not name them — and that is
# a property of THE REPOSITORY CREATED ON 5 Sep, not of the one before it.
# ===========================================================================


# ── EDITOR, OS AND MACHINE DROPPINGS ───────────────────────────────────────
# `**/.DS_Store` is in the config and is redundant here: a gitignore pattern
# with no slash already matches at every depth.
.DS_Store
.fuse_hidden*
# NOT IN THE CONFIG, AND DELIBERATE. A clone has no stray logs and no local
# notes, so the config never has to name them; this folder does.
*.log
NOTES.md
scratch/


# ── BUILD OUTPUT AND INSTALLED DEPENDENCIES ────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
dist/
.data/

# Compact build output. Regenerate with `npm run compact`.
contracts/managed/
contracts/managed-vault/
# The symlink `compile-vault.sh` makes so the vault's generated caller can find
# the account's generated module at the path it imports. Build output pointing
# at build output.
contracts/Acct
contracts/build/

# PROBE OUTPUT — THROWAWAY COMPILER RUNS CARRYING PROVING AND VERIFIER KEYS
# MEASURED IN MEGABYTES.
#
# THESE FOUR RULES USED TO NAME `contracts/probe/`, `contracts/probe4/` and
# `contracts/probe5/`, WHICH DO NOT EXIST UNDER THOSE NAMES, AND MATCHED NONE
# OF THE FOUR DIRECTORIES THAT DO. What exists and was matched by nothing:
# contracts/chainprobe/ (42 M), contracts/probe-unshielded/ (5.1 M),
# contracts/probe-branch/, contracts/probe-k/. The rule written to stop probe
# output entering history did not match the probe output on this disk.
#
# The take list is the real control — nothing ships unless it is named — and
# these rules are the second line, so they now match directories that exist
# rather than directories somebody remembered.
contracts/probe*/
contracts/chainprobe/


# ── NETWORK STATE, KEYS AND SECRETS ────────────────────────────────────────
# THE WALLET SEED THAT HOLDS THIS PROJECT'S FUNDS IS IN HERE, IN PLAIN TEXT.
# Losing it means asking the faucet again; committing it means anyone who
# reads the repository can spend those funds. A key in a published history
# cannot be taken back by any later edit, so these paths are excluded here
# rather than cleaned up afterwards. The maintainers also run a scanner over
# the whole tree before publishing, which reads every value in these files and
# refuses a tree carrying one anywhere else; that is their own tooling, it does
# not run over a contribution, and this list is the guarantee.
.midnight/
.env
.env.example
.keys-backup/
.wallet-state/
# The private state store: contract-address-keyed, rebuilt on demand, and it
# holds signer secrets in an encrypted LevelDB.
midnight-level-db/
# NOT IN THE CONFIG. A local convenience file that has never existed here and
# would carry the same values as `.env` if it did.
.env.local

# `.env.example` IS IGNORED ON A RULING AND NOT BY OVERSIGHT. It is the one
# file the scanner structurally cannot clear: it is BOTH a secret root and a
# candidate to ship, and a value is never searched for inside the file it was
# read out of — so the door reported it SUPPRESSED, `nothing below is a
# refusal and nothing below has been checked`, rather than passing it. Ruled
# 5 Sep: env files can be added incrementally later. Leave it out.


# ── EVERYTHING THE `.command` FILES LEAVE BEHIND ───────────────────────────
# Reports, stack samples and symbol maps. `REPORT-*.txt` does not match the
# bare `REPORT.txt`, so that one is named.
REPORT-*.txt
REPORT.txt
logs/

# Scratch left behind where `rm` is unavailable, and pre-reset archives. Both
# are cleared out by hand. The config names one dated archive; the glob here
# is wider on purpose — the next one will have a different date.
_to_delete/
.pre-reset-*/
.s38-scratch/

# NOT IN THE CONFIG, AND DELIBERATE. A scratch directory this working folder
# carries and a clone has no reason to.
# THE RULE IS THE DIRECTORY AND NEVER A FILENAME: this file ships, so a rule
# naming a file inside a private directory publishes that name in the act of
# excluding it.
Claude outputs/


# ── CLONED SOURCE THAT BELONGS TO SOMEBODY ELSE ────────────────────────────
# The Foundation's source and SAFE's, cloned in so this folder is
# self-contained and a scope can cite source rather than memory.
midnight-src/
/SAFE/

# ── THE WALLET AND THE LIBRARY, WHICH ARE NOW IN THIS REPOSITORY ───────────
# The folder this block used to exclude whole is gone: the wallet's source is
# `apps/wallet/`, the library it consumes is `packages/identity/`, and both are
# named on the take list file by file like everything else here. What is left
# to exclude is what those two BUILD or download, and one test fixture.
#
# THE PROVING-KEY MATERIAL IS THE LINE THAT MATTERS. It is ~117 MB, it is
# re-downloadable, and it is verified against the SHA-256 pins in
# `manifest.json` on every use - so the manifest is committed and the material
# never is.
/packages/identity/lib/
/packages/identity/dist/
/packages/identity/.vite-cache/
/apps/wallet/dist/
/apps/wallet/public/keys/*
!/apps/wallet/public/keys/manifest.json

# The stable test wallet's seed. A test fixture, nobody's money - but a seed is
# a seed, and no seed is ever committed.
/apps/wallet/test-wallet.seed

# ── AND THE SHAPES, NOT ONLY THE TWO EXACT PATHS ABOVE ─────────────────────
#
# THESE CAME ACROSS WITH THE WALLET AND ALMOST DID NOT. They lived in that
# folder's own `.gitignore`, which is now at `packages/identity/.gitignore` and
# therefore governs that subtree and nothing else - so on the day of the merge
# `apps/wallet/**` was covered for two exact filenames and for no SHAPE at all,
# and a root `.gitignore` holding no `*.pem`, no `*.key` and no `*.mnemonic`
# sat under a commit door that runs `git add -A`. Nothing occupied the gap.
# That is the point: a guard that stops covering the tree it was written for
# fails towards silence, and the next file is the one nobody looks at.
#
# UNANCHORED ON PURPOSE, ALL OF THEM. Anchoring a rule NARROWS it, and every
# rule here protects something rather than merely tidying - so matching at any
# depth is the safer error, wherever a rule protects something.
*.pem
*.key
*.mnemonic
*.p12
seed*.txt
*.seed
id_rsa
id_ed25519

# AND THE TEN THAT WERE MISSING FROM THAT LIST, ADDED 7 Sep. Every one of them
# is a container for a private key, a seed or a passphrase, and every one was
# measured against the take list first: none of them matches a file that ships,
# so nothing is being excluded that anybody meant to publish.
#
# THE PUBLIC HALVES ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT. A certificate and a public key are
# meant to be read, and a rule excluding them is a rule somebody later deletes
# for being wrong - taking the rest of this list with it.
#
# THE SHAPES THIS LIST NAMES ARE NOT CHECKED ANYWHERE IN THIS REPOSITORY.
# A rule that is later anchored, or reached back over by a negation written
# below it, covers less than it reads as covering and nothing here says so.
# Read a rule against the shape it is meant to catch before trusting it.
#
*.pfx
*.p8
*.jks
*.keystore
*.kdbx
*.ppk
*.asc
*.gpg
id_ecdsa
id_dsa

# `.env.*` AND NOT `.env` OR `.env.example`, BECAUSE THIS FILE ALREADY DECIDES
# BOTH OF THOSE ABOVE AND GIT TAKES THE LAST MATCHING RULE. The wallet's own
# ignore file carried `!.env.example` - an un-ignore - and copying that line
# here would have reached back over this repository's line 93 and started
# TRACKING a file it deliberately excludes. Only the pattern that is genuinely
# missing is added.
.env.*

# The wallet's dependency cache, which its config deliberately keeps out of
# node_modules: a cache is not a dependency.
/.vite-cache/

# The wallet's own working record, moved here when its folder stopped existing.
# Working documents, never part of the software - the same rule the rest of
# `docs/` is held to.
docs/wallet/


# ── THE THREE FILES A COMMIT IS BUILT FROM, WHICH A COMMIT MUST NOT CARRY ──
# What it says, what it contains, where it goes. Each is rewritten whole for
# every commit, so tracking one would put the last commit's version inside the
# next commit — the message about the message.
COMMIT-MESSAGE.md
COMMIT-FILES.txt
COMMIT-BRANCH.txt
# The record PUBLIC-REPO-APPLY.command writes when it has run. It describes one
# machine at one moment, like a report.
PUBLIC-REPO-APPLIED.txt

# THE TWO OTHER FILES A DOOR READS AT THE ROOT, NAMED 18 Sep (CT15).
# Both were on NO list at all - neither ignored here nor allowlisted - which is
# the one state this file and the take list exist together to make impossible,
# and they were found by measuring rather than by reading. ADMIT-REASON.txt is
# the sentence a person writes to justify admitting files to the language floor;
# BACKUP-PROCESS-FILES.txt is the list the backup door reads. Both describe this
# working copy at one moment, exactly like the three above them.
ADMIT-REASON.txt
BACKUP-PROCESS-FILES.txt


# ── THE MIGRATION'S OWN TOOLING AND ITS TWO LISTS ──────────────────────────
# The config describes this process, and the take list is a decision record
# with our own reasoning in it. Neither is software.
PUBLIC-REPO-CONFIG.json
PUBLIC-REPO-TAKE-LIST.txt


# ── THE `.command` FILES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# v1 ships none of them. They stay in this folder exactly where they are and
# keep working; they are simply never committed.
#
# THE COST IS REAL AND IT IS WRITTEN DOWN RATHER THAN DISCOVERED: 116 small
# contract programs and 130 deliberate breakages live inside those files and
# nowhere else in this folder. Moving that work into `scripts/` is its own
# round. If it does not happen, this rule is where the loss began.
*.command

# ── THE MAINTAINER'S OWN MACHINERY, EXCLUDED BY ONE RULE ───────────────────
# Everything this project uses to BUILD, CHECK, PUBLISH and GOVERN itself, in
# one directory, ignored whole: the publication tooling, the language floor and
# its guards, the commit doors' rules, the board's checkers, the mutation
# harnesses, and two research probes.
#
# IT IS ONE LINE BECAUSE IT IS ONE FACT. Until 18 Sep this file named 34 of
# those files by hand, one at a time, and `.git/info/exclude` named 20 more —
# 54 lines all restating that private machinery was sitting inside `scripts/`,
# a published directory. **BOOKKEEPING THAT LONG GOES WRONG, AND IT HAD:** four
# more files of exactly the same kind were on disk in `scripts/` and on NO list
# at all — neither ignored here nor allowlisted there, which is the one state
# this file and the take list exist together to make impossible.
#
# SO THE BOUNDARY IS THE DIRECTORY, NOT A LIST OF NAMES. A new tool is private
# because of where it is put, rather than because somebody remembered to add a
# line here afterwards. `.midnight/` above is the same shape, and until today it
# was this repository's only correct instance of it.
#
# AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT INSIDE `.midnight/`. That directory holds this
# project's wallet seeds and its signer keys in plain text; a directory people
# copy tooling out of must not be the directory that holds the money.
.maintainer/


