# ── THE SEVEN DOCUMENTS THAT SHIPPED AND SHOULD NOT HAVE ───────────────────
# THE THREE RULES BELOW NAME FILES THAT WERE ALREADY IN THE INDEX WHEN THOSE
# RULES WERE WRITTEN, AND IGNORING A TRACKED FILE DOES NOTHING ABOUT THAT.
# The three scripts among the ten are a different case: they left the index by
# being MOVED under `.maintainer/` above, and nothing under `.maintainer/` has
# ever been tracked.
# UNTRACK-SHIPPED-BY-MISTAKE.command TOOK THE TEN OUT OF THE INDEX AND LEFT
# EVERY ONE OF THEM ON DISK. Nothing under docs/ is in the index now, so these
# rules do what every other rule here does: they keep a file out rather than
# argue with one that is already in. That door's fixed list has since been
# re-pinned to a later set of fourteen: none of the ten is among them, and
# none of the fourteen is tracked any longer either. Every other rule here is
# honoured from the first commit because the fresh `init` was built from the
# take list.
#
# docs/design/ is a GENERATED OUTPUT DIRECTORY rather than written
# documentation. Everything under it is derived from the compiled contracts,
# and edges.json is 199 KB of machine JSON that records a count of internal files.
#
# docs/handoff/ is six documents written for an internal successor — "You are
# picking up a system that moves other people's salaries" — that CITE
# DOCUMENTS WHICH DO NOT SHIP: docs/midnight/, docs/scope-identity.md,
# BACKLOG.md. A reader following them reaches nothing. identity-brief.md
# describes the OTHER repository and shipped by default.
#
# BOTH ARE KEPT ON DISK BECAUSE BOTH ARE USED HERE: the generated design
# output is the working copy of a derived description, and docs/handoff/ is the map a person reads.
docs/design/
docs/design/edges.json
docs/handoff/

# docs/decisions/ is thirteen numbered records of choices made while building
# this system. They are kept on disk because they are still read here, and they
# are not published yet because they are written in shorthand and numbered
# rather than titled, so a newcomer opening 0004 learns very little. They go
# out together, once, when they have titles somebody can read.
#
# THE FOLDER IS THE RULE AND NOT THIRTEEN FILE NAMES. Naming each file here
# would publish thirteen names to say that thirteen files are private, which is
# the opposite of the intent. It is also what an earlier change left undone:
# the records were taken out of the published set and given no rule at all, so
# they sat on disk belonging to neither list. That is the one state this file
# and the published file list exist together to prevent, and it cost a merge
# the same afternoon - changing branch could not write eleven files that were
# already sitting there unclaimed, and the tool reported a failure that had in
# fact already succeeded.
docs/decisions/


# ── THE AGENT DEFINITIONS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `.claude/agents/*.md` describe HOW work is checked here rather than WHAT the
# software does — the same class as CLAUDE.md above and the same reason.
# Ruled 5 Sep: not in v1, decided later. Adding a file after publication is
# easy; removing one is impossible.
.claude/


# ── THE BROWSER-ONLY BUILD, DECIDED AGAINST 9 Sep, STILL PUBLISHED ─────────
# It ran the whole product in a browser with no server, against a simulated
# ledger. The product runs against a chain now, and a build with no filesystem
# cannot read a contract address, an indexer, a node or a proof server - so it
# has nothing to select. IT WAS NOT TAKEN OFF: `src/standalone/` and
# `vite.standalone.config.ts` are still in the index and still named by
# PUBLIC-REPO-TAKE-LIST.txt, and so is `browser-proving/`. It stays on disk,
# it still builds, and it is used again the day it selects the chain for
# itself.
#
# THE TWO COMMENTED RULES BELOW NAME PATHS THAT ARE ALREADY IN THE INDEX, so
# they do not remove them: ignoring a tracked file does nothing at all.
# NEITHER DOES UNTRACK-SHIPPED-BY-MISTAKE.command, whose fixed list names
# neither path. Taking them out means changing that list first, and they stay
# on disk where they work.
#
# HELD, DELIBERATELY, AND THE TWO LINES ARE COMMENTED RATHER THAN DELETED SO
# THE DECISION IS VISIBLE. Taking the browser-only build off the published set
# is TWO acts: the lists stop naming it, and the paths leave the index.
# Ignoring a tracked path achieves neither. Both are done together, in their
# own change, and the tool that takes paths out of the index needs its own
# fixed list updated first - it refuses any set but the one it was written
# for, so that a mis-edited list cannot make it remove the wrong thing.
# src/standalone/
# vite.standalone.config.ts
